= Using the discussion tools =

<code>mediawiki-discussion-tools.el</code> browses any MediaWiki discussion page: a
talk page, a village pump desk, a noticeboard, or the support desk.  It lists the
threads in a table, opens a thread in a window below the list, and posts replies,
new threads and resolution markers through the API.  Nothing in it is specific to
one wiki - the page name is an argument.

Two worked examples ship with the package:

* The '''support desk''' at
  [https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Project:Support_desk Project:Support desk] on
  mediawiki.org, wrapped by <code>mediawiki-support-desk</code>.
* The '''Wikipedia Village pump''' at
  [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Village_pump Wikipedia:Village pump],
  written up in
  [https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/VILLAGE-PUMP.md VILLAGE-PUMP.md]
  after being tested against en.wikipedia.org.

The design behind the module, and what was deliberately left out of it, is in
[https://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/specs/DISCUSSION-TOOLS-PLAN.md DISCUSSION-TOOLS-PLAN.md].

== Opening a page ==

<pre>
 M-x mediawiki-discussion-tools RET Page name RET
 M-x mediawiki-support-desk
</pre>

<code>mediawiki-discussion-tools</code> prompts for the page and, when no
<code>mediawiki-site</code> is set, for the site.  <code>mediawiki-support-desk</code>
is a shortcut for <code>Project:Support desk</code>; set
<code>mediawiki-support-desk-page</code> to point it at a different page.

Open the page that actually holds the threads, not a hub page that transcludes the
desks.  The Village pump index has no threads of its own, so the list comes up
empty there - open a desk subpage such as
<code>Wikipedia:Village pump (policy)</code> instead.

The list is shown in <code>*MW Discussion: PAGE*</code>.  Threads come from the
DiscussionTools extension, so the wiki must have it installed.

== The thread list ==

The list has five columns:

{| class="wikitable"
! Width !! Column !! Contents
|-
| 3 || || Status icon (see below)
|-
| 40 || Title || The thread heading, with markup stripped
|-
| 16 || Author || Whoever opened the thread
|-
| 5 || # || Number of comments
|-
| 20 || Last || How long ago the last comment was posted
|}

The Title and Author widths are minimums: they grow to fill the window, so a
wide frame shows more of a long heading instead of leaving empty space.  The
table refits itself when you press <kbd>g</kbd> and whenever the window or frame
is resized.

Threads are sorted by priority, not by date; there is no header sort:

# '''Needs attention''' (<code>!</code>) - you posted, and someone answered after
  your last comment.
# '''Watching''' (<code>*</code>) - you posted and nobody has answered since.
# '''Active''' (<code>&middot;</code>) - any other thread with replies.
# '''Unanswered''' (<code>?</code>) - the opening post has no replies.
# '''Resolved''' (<code>&#x2713;</code>) - the section contains <code>{{Resolved}}</code>.
# '''Stale''' (<code>~</code>) - no activity for
  <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools-stale-days</code> days (14 by default).

The first two tiers only appear when mediawiki.el knows who you are: they need the
per-comment authors, which cost a larger list payload.  Anonymous browsing gets the
other four tiers and a lighter fetch.

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Action
|-
| <kbd>g</kbd> || Re-fetch the thread list
|-
| <kbd>RET</kbd> || Show the thread at point below the list
|-
| <kbd>n</kbd> / <kbd>p</kbd> || Next / previous thread
|-
| <kbd>m</kbd> / <kbd>C</kbd> || Start a new thread
|-
| <kbd>r</kbd> || Reply to the thread at point
|-
| <kbd>d</kbd> || Mark the thread resolved
|-
| <kbd>s</kbd> || Search this page and its archives
|}

The keys follow Gnus where Gnus has an equivalent, so a newsgroup habit carries
over.

== Reading a thread ==

<kbd>RET</kbd> opens the thread in <code>*MW Thread View*</code>, a single buffer
below the list that is reused for every thread.  Each comment shows its author and
timestamp, with replies indented one level per answer.  <kbd>n</kbd> and
<kbd>p</kbd> walk the list and replace the view contents, so paging through a desk
costs nothing.

Comment bodies are rendered as HTML rather than flattened to text, so links stay
clickable, images are displayed as thumbnails, and lists and paragraphs keep their
shape.  <kbd>C-c C-o</kbd> opens the link at point: a link to a page on the current
site opens in an Emacs buffer, anything else goes to your browser.  <kbd>mouse-1</kbd>
on a link opens it in the browser, and <kbd>RET</kbd> still closes the view - it is
deliberately not bound to the link.  <kbd>TAB</kbd> moves point to the next link in
the thread and <kbd>S-TAB</kbd> back to the previous one, wherever point happens to
be.

<kbd>e</kbd> edits the post at point.  Because the API can replace a whole section
but not a single comment, the comment's wikitext is cut out of the thread's
section and shown alone in <code>*MW Edit Post*</code>; saving writes the section
back with the other comments untouched, and refuses if the page changed
underneath.  If the comment cannot be located (an unsigned comment, or a
signature the parser does not recognise), the page opens at the thread's heading
so it can still be edited by hand.

<kbd>&</kbd> opens the comment at point in your web browser.  Every comment's
DiscussionTools id is also the anchor MediaWiki gives it, so the browser lands on
that comment rather than the top of the page - useful for seeing a comment in
context, following a link inside it, or passing it to someone else.

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Action
|-
| <kbd>r</kbd> || Reply to this thread
|-
| <kbd>e</kbd> || Edit the post at point
|-
| <kbd>d</kbd> || Mark this thread resolved
|-
| <kbd>&</kbd> || Open the comment at point in your web browser
|-
| <kbd>C-c C-o</kbd> || Open the link at point (wiki pages in Emacs)
|-
| <kbd>TAB</kbd> / <kbd>S-TAB</kbd> || Next / previous link in the thread
|-
| <kbd>g</kbd> || Re-fetch and re-render this thread
|-
| <kbd>n</kbd> / <kbd>p</kbd> || Next / previous thread
|-
| <kbd>s</kbd> || Search this page and its archives
|-
| <kbd>RET</kbd> / <kbd>q</kbd> || Close the view and return to the list
|}

== Replying ==

<kbd>r</kbd> opens <code>*MW Reply*</code> with a header naming the thread and a
separator line, <code>--- Reply below this line ---</code>.  Type below the
separator; anything above it, including the separator, is discarded.

<pre>
 C-c C-c    post the reply and refresh the list
 C-c C-k    cancel
</pre>

The reply is indented relative to the comment it answers, not always with a single
colon: answering a <code>:</code> comment produces <code>::</code>, and answering a
<code>*</code> vote produces <code>*:</code> so the reply stays inside the list
item.  A section with no indented comment falls back to <code>:</code>.  The
signature from <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools-signature</code> (default
<code>~~~~</code>) is appended on its own line.

== Starting a new thread ==

<kbd>m</kbd> (or <kbd>C</kbd>) prompts for a title and opens
<code>*MW New Thread*</code>, which works exactly like the reply buffer:
<kbd>C-c C-c</kbd> posts, <kbd>C-c C-k</kbd> cancels.  The body is not indented and
gets the signature appended, as a new section should.  Nothing is published from
the title prompt alone.

== Marking a thread resolved ==

<kbd>d</kbd> asks for an optional summary and appends a comment at the thread's
indentation level:

* with a summary: <code>{{Resolved|the answer above fixed it|~~~~}}</code>
* without one: <code>{{Resolved|~~~~}}</code>

The summary is a good place to point at the reply that solved the problem.  Either
way the thread comes back from the next refresh with a <code>&#x2713;</code>, because
the list reads the section wikitext and looks for the <code>{{Resolved}}</code>
template.

'''The template name is fixed.'''  A wiki that uses, say, <code>{{Done}}</code> or
<code>{{Fixed}}</code> will still receive <code>{{Resolved}}</code> and the list
will not recognise it.  Check what the desk you are using actually marks
resolutions with before relying on the tick.

== Searching a page and its archives ==

<kbd>s</kbd> asks for a search term and runs one prefixed search over the page and
everything under it, so archived threads are found without opening an archive page:

* <code>fonts prefix:Project:Support desk</code> finds the live desk and
  <code>Project:Support desk/Archive 24</code> alike.
* An '''empty term''' lists every subpage, which is the quickest way to find the
  archive pages.

Results appear in <code>*MW Discussion Search*</code> with HTML stripped; <kbd>RET</kbd>
opens the page at point.  The search goes through the wiki's search backend, so a
wiki whose backend does not implement <code>prefix:</code> will return ordinary
full-text hits instead of a title-restricted set.

== Archived threads ==

Threads leave the live page when a bot archives them.  The list remembers what it
showed last time and says so on refresh:

<pre>
 Discussion refreshed (12 threads); 3 disappeared (probably archived -
 s to search archives)
</pre>

That is the whole of the module's archive handling: it does not chase threads into
archive pages.  Use <kbd>s</kbd> to find them when you need one.

== Options ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Option !! Default !! Meaning
|-
| <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools-stale-days</code> || 14 || Days without activity before a thread counts as stale; match the desk's archiving threshold
|-
| <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools-max-threads</code> || <code>nil</code> || Show at most this many threads; <code>nil</code> shows all
|-
| <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools-signature</code> || <code>~~~~</code> || Appended to replies, new threads and resolution markers
|-
| <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools-reply-separator</code> || <code>--- Reply below this line ---</code> || Text above this line is discarded when posting
|}

All of them live in the <code>mediawiki-discussion-tools</code> customization group.

== Authentication and what it changes ==

Reading a desk needs no login.  Posting does: <code>mediawiki-site-get-token</code>
must return a CSRF token, which means clientlogin or OAuth is configured (see
[http://github.com/hexmode/mediawiki-el/blob/master/README.mediawiki README.mediawiki]).
Without it, posting fails with <code>No CSRF token - authenticate first</code>.

Knowing your user name also switches on the two attention tiers, because replies
have to be scanned for your comments and for anyone who followed them.  The name
comes from the site configuration, or from one <code>meta=userinfo</code> call for
OAuth sessions that do not store it.

== Troubleshooting ==

; No threads found on PAGE
: The page has no level-2 headings, or you opened a hub page that only transcludes
  the real desks.  Open the desk subpage.

; Cannot determine section number for thread
: The heading changed between the list fetch and the edit, so its anchor no longer
  matches the table of contents.  Press <kbd>g</kbd> and try again.

; Reply failed / Resolve failed
: The API's own message is shown - an edit conflict, an abuse filter, or a protected
  page.  Nothing was written in those cases.

; No matches in search
: Check the term, then try an empty term to confirm that the page's subpages are
  visible at all.  A wiki without <code>prefix:</code> support ignores the title
  restriction rather than failing.

== What it does not do ==

* Flow / Structured Discussions boards: those are not wikitext sections.
* Editing or deleting individual comments; the browser posts, it does not rewrite
  history.
* Watching a desk from inside Emacs; use <code>mediawiki-watchlist</code>.
* More than one site at a time: the buffer is tied to the site it was opened on.

